\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Schematic of a server tick (illustrative timings, not measured data). Each tick the server receives client input, updates the world, and sends a snapshot; at 20\nobreakspace  {}Hz it has 50\nobreakspace  {}ms for all of it. If the work per tick grows with the number of clients and passes 50\nobreakspace  {}ms (panel\nobreakspace  {}C), the snapshot still leaves at the 50\nobreakspace  {}ms deadline, because the next tick's work has to start. The tick rate therefore stays near 20\nobreakspace  {}Hz, but the work is unfinished, so the snapshots carry less up-to-date information and clients see worse ping and more gaps.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tickvisual}{{1}{3}{Schematic of a server tick (illustrative timings, not measured data). Each tick the server receives client input, updates the world, and sends a snapshot; at 20~Hz it has 50~ms for all of it. If the work per tick grows with the number of clients and passes 50~ms (panel~C), the snapshot still leaves at the 50~ms deadline, because the next tick's work has to start. The tick rate therefore stays near 20~Hz, but the work is unfinished, so the snapshots carry less up-to-date information and clients see worse ping and more gaps.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
